PARS = [
("DSSs exist for parts of this task", """DSSs exist for parts of this task {5}. Spread simulators forecast fire behavior from fuel, terrain, and weather {6;7}. Allocation tools assign crews and aircraft against a forecast {8;9}. Integrated platforms bring these parts together for use in the field {4;10}. However, field studies of these tools measure how widely they are used, not whether the decisions taken with them are better {11;12}. A critical review of DSS research finds the same pattern across the field, many systems built and few shown to help {13}. VODA is therefore measured as the change in the outcome of the same incidents, with and without the system."""),
("Three structural limits exist across these systems", """Three structural limits exist across these systems. First, the alternatives are predefined. A rule base, a menu of tactics, or an optimization model fixes the orders before the incident, so the system can only rank what it is given {10}. Decision analysis has long identified the generation of alternatives as the weakest step of the process {14;15;16}. The methods proposed for it either enumerate variations of a fixed model {17} or depend on the recall of the operator under time pressure {18}. Second, uncertainty is representable only through prior distributions or belief intervals. Real-time sensing cannot supply them {19;20}, and the reasoning behind a recommendation is exposed through post-hoc attribution {21}. Third, integrated platforms embed the forecast and the decision logic in one tool {4;10}. Hence, neither is validated on its own, and the benefit of a decision mechanism cannot be separated from the quality of the forecast behind it."""),
("VODA answers the first limit", """VODA answers the first limit with an open decision space, which is its central contribution. The set of orders is not fixed. It grows during the incident, under verification by the hazard model. The decision core has two layers. The closed layer is a self-tuning fuzzy doctrine that maps decision concepts to intervention families through a sparse rule base. When the standing decision falls short, the open layer lets a pretrained large language model (LLM) propose a new rule, a blend of existing interventions, or a new intervention composed from physical effects the simulator can execute. Nothing enters the doctrine unless it passes a chain of algebraic gates and a paired forecast test. Hence, the space is closed at physical effects and open at interventions, and its invariants are proved."""),
("The third limit is answered", """The third limit is answered by the separation of two cores. A simulation core owns the hazard state, and a decision core reads it through confidence-rated observations. The simulation core is validated by blind hindcast against satellite records of documented fires, and the decision core by paired comparison on that validated core, so the benefit of opening the decision space is read as a difference on identical worlds. The decision core is distributed over regional agents, decides in a small fraction of a second, and its cost grows slowly with the number of agents, so it suits real-time operation."""),
("The remainder of the article", """Section II reviews related work, Section III presents the architecture, Section IV the decision core, Section V the results, Section VI the discussion, and Section VII the conclusion."""),
("Mechanisms that generate alternatives share one pattern", """Mechanisms that generate alternatives share one pattern. Some search a fixed model for structurally different solutions {17}. Some combine modules under compatibility constraints {29}. Some add new actions to the action model at run time {30;31}. In every case the new alternatives are composed from parts the system already understands, so an open decision space is an open composition over a closed and verified basis. Mechanisms that change a rule base at run time follow the same pattern. Evolving TS models tune and add rules within the vocabulary {25;26}. Value-based selectors learn when a structural change is needed {32}. Generative models draft rules outside the current structure {33}. VODA uses all three, with tuning and selection in the closed layer and generation in the open layer, behind one satisficing acceptance rule {34} that keeps generation cheap to reject and the evaluator independent of the generator."""),
]
